Statements:
[
    "Retriever failures are categorized into three types.",
    "The three failure types are coverage/completeness, relevancy, and ranking/order.",
    "Coverage/completeness is about how much relevant context the retriever returns.",
    "The metric for coverage/completeness is contextual recall.",
    "Chunk size can be increased if recall is low.",
    "Chunk overlap and overall chunking strategy are tunable parameters.",
    "Top-k can be increased to retrieve more chunks.",
    "A stronger embedding model can improve coverage/completeness.",
    "Chunking and embedding stages directly affect retriever recall.",
    "Relevancy is about how relevant the retrieved results are to the query.",
    "The metric for relevancy is contextual relevancy.",
    "Chunk size can be decreased if relevancy is low.",
    "Chunk overlap and chunking method are tunable parameters for relevancy.",
    "A better semantic embedding model can improve relevancy.",
    "Top-k can be reduced or filtered to remove irrelevant items.",
    "Chunking and embedding should be adjusted to improve semantic match between query and chunks.",
    "Ranking/order is about whether the most relevant items are ranked highest.",
    "The metric for ranking/order is precision.",
    "Precision is often targeted by a reranker.",
    "A reranker can be added or tuned to re-score results and improve order.",
    "Top-k can be increased to provide more candidates for the reranker.",
    "The embedding model in the retriever affects initial ordering.",
    "Chunking parameters can affect which items are available to be ranked.",
    "The retriever is usually a bi-encoder.",
    "A reranker, which is a cross-encoder, helps improve final ranking.",
    "Metrics should be applied together across the app.",
    "Parameters should be tuned component-wise depending on which metric or component is weak.",
    "Chunking hyperparameters and the embedding stage are the main levers for retriever behavior."
] 

